  <p>Both legs cramped 400 m into the swim and never fully released for the next six and a half hours. Every split was missed by a wide margin for the same reason.</p>

  <p>A few weeks on, two things from that day are worth writing down. One is that I still cannot fully explain the cramping, though I have narrowed it. The other is that I came close to stopping four times, none of the four had anything to do with the pain, and after the second transition there was not a fifth.</p>

  <h2>Before</h2>

  <p>Two weeks out I pinched a nerve in my shoulder. It cost me a handful of sessions, not many. Going in, that was the thing I was worried about — and it was the only thing that behaved.</p>

  <p>Race morning was clean. I slept badly but not disastrously, ate the same boring breakfast I had eaten before every long session, racked my nutrition in T1, got into the wetsuit, swam a warm-up. Everything to that point went exactly as rehearsed.</p>

  <p>Then the start was pushed back 1:15 for a thunderstorm, and I stayed in the wetsuit for the entire delay. At the time that did not feel like a decision. It is the first thing I would change.</p>

  <p>Water and air temperature were close to perfect.</p>

  <h2>Swim — <span class="data h2num">0:58:34</span></h2>

  <p>The shoulder felt fine in the water, which was the good news and lasted about six minutes.</p>

  <p>At 400 m, both calves went. I had never had this before — not in the pool, not across five to seven open-water sessions, not in brick workouts. It alternated between legs for the rest of the swim.</p>

  <p>At around 1000 m I took a kick to the head and got dizzy. <strong>That was the first time I thought about stopping</strong>, and it was not a decision about the race. I did not know whether continuing was safe or whether I should signal for the water rescue. I kept going, but from that moment the question stopped being <em>how fast</em> and became <em>at all</em>. The last 800 m I swam almost entirely with my arms, because my legs were shut.</p>

  <p>Out of the water thirteen minutes over a target I had already downgraded once.</p>

  <h2>T1 — <span class="data h2num">0:07:01</span></h2>

  <p>Seven minutes standing still in a race where every second is normally defended. I stretched, deliberately and at length.</p>

  <p>That number is the clearest record in my splits of the moment the race changed its purpose.</p>

  <h2>Bike — <span class="data h2num">3:44:45</span></h2>

  <p>The first 5 to 10 km were flat and genuinely good. 35 km/h, no cramping, and I briefly believed the swim had been a contained problem. Then the first climb, and both calves went at once.</p>

  <p>From there, every metre of elevation gain triggered it. Calves, quads, eventually the whole lower body, and later cramping through my upper body as well. On fast flat sections where you would normally push, I rode standing up, trying to stretch anything I could reach while still moving.</p>

  <p><strong>Around kilometre 35</strong> I rode alongside someone with almost the same problems and almost the same target time. We talked. There was real sympathy there — he was having my exact day. Then he pulled out. Watching him stop hurt more than anything my legs were doing at that moment, and it was the second time I seriously considered it myself.</p>

  <p><strong>Kilometre 60</strong>, just after a steep descent I knew I would have to climb back up later. I did the arithmetic: 30 km still to ride, including that hill, and then a half marathon — the distance people train for months to run once — which had not even started yet. I cried on the bike. Mostly frustration, some pain. Third time.</p>

  <p><strong>The last 10 km</strong> were in pouring rain. Everything already hurt and then the weather turned. Fourth time.</p>

  <p>Somewhere in the middle of all this I ran the one experiment the day allowed. I was fuelling 60 g of carbohydrate per hour, tested repeatedly in training, and my stomach was fine. As the cramping got worse I pushed sodium far above plan, because that is what everyone tells you to do.</p>

  <p>It changed nothing. Not less cramping, not shorter episodes, nothing I could detect at all.</p>

  <h2>Run — <span class="data h2num">1:54:30</span></h2>

  <p>Running is my discipline. Off the bike the cramping eased enough to hold about 4:50/km for a while, until stomach cramps arrived on top of the leg cramps in the closing kilometres. The last hour was the most uncomfortable part of the entire day.</p>

  <p>From T2 onward I did not think about stopping once.</p>

