  <h2><span class="data h2num">6:52:07</span></h2>

  <p>There were several points during that day when I was not sure I could finish. Crossing the line was the only number that mattered by the end.</p>

  <h2>Why did I cramp?</h2>

  <p>Everything I measured checks out. Loading was clean, fuelling was tested and worked, sodium was planned against what I had rehearsed, conditions were mild. And the sodium I took mid-race did nothing, which is about as clean a negative as an n=1 gets.</p>

  <p>The literature helps here more than the finish-line consensus does. The electrolyte-and-dehydration explanation is weaker than its popularity suggests; the better-supported model is altered neuromuscular control under fatigue, which predicts cramping specifically when a muscle contracts in a <em>shortened</em> position and names insufficient conditioning for the specific movement as a risk factor.</p>

  <p>I trained front crawl. Because of the shoulder, I raced breaststroke — a completely different kick, loaded through the calf in exactly that position, and one I had never trained at race intensity. Both calves, at 400 m, after months without a single incident. Not proof, but it predicts the pattern I got, and the thing I actually tested mid-race does not.</p>

  <p class="note"><strong>The failure is mine and it is simple.</strong> I knew two weeks out that my shoulder might not hold, and that I might have to swim breaststroke. I did not train breaststroke. A contingency you have not rehearsed is not a contingency, it is a hope.</p>

  <h2>The four moments</h2>

  <p>Not one of them was caused by pain.</p>

  <p>The pain was roughly constant from the swim onward and got worse across the day. If pain drove quitting, the quit thoughts would have clustered at the end. They did not. They clustered where I had no idea what was still coming.</p>

  <p>The kick to the head was uncertainty about my own body. Kilometre 35 was information about my odds — someone with my exact problem and my exact goal had just demonstrated that this day ends in a DNF. Kilometre 60 was an explicit calculation about a remainder I had never covered in that state. Only the rain was really about the present getting worse.</p>

  <p>And then T2, and nothing. The pain did not drop; it got worse. What changed was the shape of what remained. A half marathon is my discipline. I have run that distance many times, I know what it costs, I know what kilometre eighteen feels like when it is going badly. There was nothing left to estimate.</p>

  <p>Six hours of an unknown remainder in an unknown state, then two hours of a known quantity in a bad state. Those are different problems, and only one of them makes you consider stopping.</p>

  <h2>What I take from it</h2>

  <p><strong>Reduce the uncertainty, not the pain.</strong> You mostly cannot reduce the pain anyway. You can almost always shrink the remainder into a unit you have already survived — next climb, next aid station. “30 km and a half marathon” is the framing that broke me at kilometre 60, and looking at the total is optional.</p>

  <p><strong>Watch what other people’s failures do to your estimate.</strong> The man at kilometre 35 gave me no new information about my legs. He gave me a vivid data point that I unconsciously treated as evidence about my own outcome, and it landed harder than any of my own symptoms.</p>

  <p><strong>Separate the feeling from the function.</strong> I cried on the bike and kept riding. Written down that sounds dramatic; the practical content is dull. How you feel about a task and whether you can execute it are two different variables, and I had been treating them as one.</p>

  <p><strong>Rehearse plan B.</strong> See above. This is the one that actually cost me the race.</p>

  <h2>What it is worth</h2>

  <p>The thought I kept having on the bike was: if I finish today, nothing in my career or my training or anywhere else will ever be too hard again.</p>

  <p>I am not going to claim that. It is one data point, and I am suspicious of large conclusions drawn from one data point, including when the data point is me.</p>

  <p>Here is what I can defend so far. In every genuinely hard session since — specifically in long runs with marathon-pace intervals — I have gone back to Kraichgau, and it works. The session in front of me is smaller than the day I already got through. That is not a feeling, it is a thing that reliably happens. It is narrow and it is real.</p>

  <p>Whether it extends to hard problems that are not endurance sport, I do not know yet. <a href="/#sport">Berlin</a> is in September. Frankfurt is next June, and by then I intend to have swum a great deal of breaststroke.</p>

  <p>What I am sure of is smaller than what I wanted to be sure of on the bike. I would rather report the smaller thing accurately.</p>
